docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:153:\caption{Registered verdict of the first test (unchanged), on \evone{40} fresh seeds at $\beta = \evone{0.05}$ and the median position. Bounds are one-sided: \evone{95}\% for the rule clause and the absolute part, \evone{98.75}\% for the comparisons. The amended pass also requires the H2 rule clause and admission of the comparison bounds, whose interval procedures were admitted on development distributions with B3 as proxy; nominal coverage for the selected R3 is not established.}\label{tab:verdict1}
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:163:\paragraph{Questions.} Q1: does R3 ($F_{111}$) leave a lower or higher identity-replacement gain than C$^*$, at the fixed pair? Q2: what do the priority phase, the residual targeting, and the pacing contribute, alone and together? Q3: do the results hold against X7-SC, X5-AG/v2, the defence-aware sealed family H4, welfare attacks, and coalitions, and does R4 repair the reserve collapse? Q4: does the first test's comparison of B3 and R3 under D2+H3 replicate on fresh markets? Q1 and Q4 are confirmatory; Q2 and Q3 are descriptive.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:176:Q1 & $d = g_{C^*} - g_{F_{111}}$, pooled, for D2+H3 and X7-SC. Direction: Holm's procedure~\cite{holm1979simple} on calibrated two-sided $p$-values for $d = \const{0}$. Size: the calibrated Bonferroni interval $I$ for $d$. Categories, in order: \emph{materially lower} if $I$ lies above $\const{0.05}$ (R3 lower) or below $\const{-0.05}$ (C$^*$ lower); \emph{equivalent} if $I$ lies within $[\const{-0.05}, \const{0.05}]$, with a direction label when Holm rejects $d = \const{0}$; \emph{ordered, size not established} if Holm rejects and neither applies; otherwise \emph{inconclusive}. A rule is called better only with the first category in its favour and a paired gross-efficiency difference (that rule minus the other) whose nominal one-sided \const{97.5}\% lower bound is at least $\const{-0.01}$ (descriptive; no calibrated joint guarantee). If that lower bound is below $\const{-0.01}$, the gain category stands and the result is labelled ``efficiency non-inferiority unresolved''; it is labelled ``lower gain with an efficiency loss'' only if the corresponding upper bound is below $\const{-0.01}$. \\
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:178:Q2, Q3 & Registered analyses descriptive, with nominal intervals and extrema selected on training data; the post hoc lottery analysis selects its companion and weight on evaluation data. \\\bottomrule
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:189:The registered estimates and decisions below come from the report that the tagged code wrote; the addendum beside it (Section~\ref{sec:design}) adds labels and disclosures. Tables~\ref{tab:ph-q1}, \ref{tab:ph-q4}, \ref{tab:ph-welfare}, and~\ref{tab:ph-welfare-sens} report exploratory analyses of existing records, and Tables~\ref{tab:ph-stability} and~\ref{tab:ph-q4-stability} the separate registered-configuration stability check; none of these changes a registered estimate or decision. Gains are normalised attacker gains; intervals are those of Table~\ref{tab:rules-v2}. Some descriptive fixed-reference contrasts on fresh1 (\site{r_ext_n} interval entries in the report's per-market Q2 rows) have numerically extreme studentized intervals: the reference-profit contrast there is above rounding scale on only \site{r_ext_seeds} of \site{r_ext_total} seeds (the others are at most \site{r_ext_round} in absolute value), and \site{r_ext_cnt} of the \site{r_ext_tot} frozen resamples (\site{r_ext_share}) omit all three, so their standard error is of rounding size and their bootstrap-t statistics are huge. We retain these frozen outputs, list them in the audit file, and draw no inferential conclusion from them; no other interval in the report has a bound beyond $\const{1000}$ in absolute value.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:191:\paragraph{Age-ranked versus history-based checking (Q1).} Pooled over the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches leave the identity-replacement attacker a measured gain against C$^*$ higher than against R3 by \site{r_q1_d_est} under D2+H3 and by \site{r_q1_x_est} under X7-SC. Both direction tests reject $d = \const{0}$ (calibrated input $p' = $ \site{r_q1_d_p}; Holm-adjusted $p = $ \site{r_q1_holm} for each). Both intervals lie inside the registered margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$: the categories are ``\site{r_q1_d_cat}'' (D2+H3) and ``\site{r_q1_x_cat}'' (X7-SC), and the separate efficiency label is ``\site{r_q1_d_eff}''. R3 is not materially better than the development-selected comparator. The difference changes sign across markets: per market (descriptive), it ranges from \site{r_q1_f3_d} on fresh3 to \site{r_q1_f1_d} on fresh1 under D2+H3. Both decisions are about the prescribed search procedure: the D2+H3 test uses search-flagged cells, and the X7-SC test uses them through its references (addendum); their nominal error guarantees are not established on the fresh, selected-rule distributions (Section~\ref{sec:design}). The paired descriptive differences in Table~\ref{tab:q1-side} are small for efficiency, audit spending, refunds on false audit failures, and the market service fraction; the reference-profit column is in raw profit units. Splitting $d$ with the same normalisation into the attacked-profit term and the reference-profit term (Table~\ref{tab:ph-q1}, exploratory) shows that almost all of it is attacked profit: the attacker earns more under C$^*$ when it deviates, not when it is truthful.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:205:\caption{Q1 side evidence, R3 minus C$^*$, pooled over the fresh markets (descriptive).}\label{tab:q1-side}
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:213:\paragraph{Repeating the comparison on six new markets (Q4).} The registered B3--R3 gain-and-efficiency comparison under D2+H3 passes on \site{r_q4_k} of the six markets (Table~\ref{tab:q4}), and all three passes (fresh1, fresh3, fresh4) are about the prescribed search procedure. On every market the estimated B3 minus R3 gain difference is positive and R3 keeps its efficiency condition; where the comparison fails, the difference is smaller than the registered threshold, not reversed: \site{r_q4_f0} on fresh0, \site{r_q4_f2} on fresh2, and \site{r_q4_f5} on fresh5, against \site{r_q4_f1} on fresh1. R3's efficiency on fresh2, the lowest, is \site{r_q4_f2_eff}. The decisions on fresh0, fresh1, fresh3, and fresh4 are about the prescribed search procedure (fresh0 and fresh3 through their references; addendum). The registered prediction of at least five passing markets failed. Q4 does not test the complete amended claim of the first test. Table~\ref{tab:ph-q4} (exploratory) decomposes each market's gain difference, which is mostly attacked profit. R3's reported audit spending is about five percent of the realised first-best surplus, and the market's service fraction (all jobs served over the first-best number) is near one. The three non-passing markets have smaller attacked-profit reductions; these descriptive diagnostics do not identify their cause.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:234:\caption{Q2 under D2+H3: factorial contrasts, searched and fixed attacks, with attack and reference terms and efficiency (descriptive, nominal intervals).}\label{tab:q2}
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:247:\paragraph{Stronger attacks and where R3 fails (Q3).} Table~\ref{tab:q3-envelope} gives each core rule's attack envelope: the family with the highest training gain, evaluated unchanged. These are finite-search results, not bounds on the families. Against B3 the selected envelope reaches \site{r_q3_f000_f1} on fresh1; against R3 it is \site{r_q3_f111_f1} there, and against C$^*$ \site{r_q3_cs_f1}, from \site{r_q3_cs_f1_fam}. H4 was written in a separate session against the frozen defenders and committed by its hash before the lock; its author's containment note states that it contains D2+H3 exactly as a special case, so it extends the public family rather than offering a disjoint attack concept. Evaluated at the headline market only, its selected search gains \site{r_q3_f111_h4} against R3 and \site{r_q3_f000_h4} against B3. Robustness against D2+H3 is unanswered for every core rule because of search flags, and so is R3's robustness against X7-SC; X5-AG/v2 and H4 carry no search-quality claim. Welfare attacks are the clearest weakness. At the headline market, the selected W0 attacks (no profit constraint) yield gross efficiency \site{r_q3_w0_f000} for B3 and \site{r_q3_w0} for R3, with gains relative to the truthful reference of \site{r_q3_w0g_f000} and \site{r_q3_w0g_f111}; C$^*$ is like R3. Under the prescribed W1 searches (mean gain at least zero), the selected feasible attacks yield efficiency \site{r_q3_w1} for R3, while B3 stays at \site{r_q3_w1_f000} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-welfare}). These finite-search results are not lower bounds on efficiency over a broader policy class. An episode-level mixture, which picks a welfare attack or a profitable attack at the start of each episode, satisfies a mean-gain constraint only when its probability-weighted component gains satisfy that constraint; we examine such mixtures on the existing evaluation records. On the records used to select them, W0 weights of \site{r_ph_w_r3} for R3 and \site{r_ph_w_cs} for C$^*$, with the H4 attack as companion, give estimated normalised gains of \site{r_ph_g_r3} and \site{r_ph_g_cs} and gross efficiencies of \site{r_ph_e_r3} and \site{r_ph_e_cs}; with the public D2+H3 attack as companion the efficiencies are \site{r_ph_e_r3_d} and \site{r_ph_e_cs_d} (Tables~\ref{tab:ph-welfare} and~\ref{tab:ph-welfare-sens}). The registered W1 constraint is a non-negative mean gain on the training seeds. Reconstructed from the search archives, the training mean gains of these mixtures, each at its rule--companion pair's weight in Table~\ref{tab:ph-welfare}, are \site{r_ph_t_r3} and \site{r_ph_t_r3_d} for R3 and \site{r_ph_t_cs} and \site{r_ph_t_cs_d} for C$^*$ (H4 and D2+H3 companions): three meet the training constraint, but C$^*$'s public-family mixture does not. At its evaluation-selected weight of \site{r_ph_wd_cs}, C$^*$'s public-family mixture fails the training constraint. At \site{r_ph_fw_cs}, the largest weight on the same grid at which both the training mean gain and the evaluation bound are non-negative, it has training gain \site{r_ph_ft_cs}, estimated evaluation gain \site{r_ph_fg_cs}, and gross efficiency \site{r_ph_fe_cs}, against \site{r_ph_w1_cs} under the registered W1 search (last row of Table~\ref{tab:ph-welfare}). Both rules therefore show substantial empirical efficiency losses with public-family mixtures alone; H4 is not needed for a drop of this size, and population profitability remains unestablished. The evaluation-selected weights are the largest at which a nominal one-sided \const{95} percent normal bound on the mean gain stays non-negative (the training-feasible row also requires a non-negative training mean); that bound is not adjusted for selection, the weight and the companion family were chosen on the evaluation seeds, and the bound is close to zero at the selected weights, so this checks the policy class and is not an out-of-sample estimate. The mixture efficiencies stay above \const{0.90}; the analysis does not touch Q4 or its registered predicate. For coalitions of two synchronised members, the selected total gains are \site{r_q3_coal_f000} against B3 and \site{r_q3_coal_f111_c2} against R3 (search flagged); for five, \site{r_q3_coal_f000_c5} and \site{r_q3_coal_f111} (total objective), while the policy selected for the minimum-member objective leaves R3's worst member a mean gain of \site{r_q3_coal_f111_c5min}; the decoy-and-beneficiary family gives \site{r_q3_db_f000} against B3 and \site{r_q3_db_f111} against R3. These comparisons establish neither coalition stability nor an ordering across every coalition family (Table~\ref{tab:q3-coalitions}). At $\rho = \const{1}$, where R3's terminal reserve collapses, R3 leaves \site{r_rep_f111} under D2+H3; the repair R4 lowers this to \site{r_rep_r4} and C$^*$ with the capped schedule (C$^*$/S2) to \site{r_rep_cs2} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-repair}).
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:250:\caption{Exploratory, after the report: at the headline market, the efficiency of the selected W1 and W0 attacks, and, for each companion family, the episode-level lottery between the W0 attack and a profitable attack of the same rule (two displayed companions, H4 and D2+H3; all five examined companions are in \texttt{posthoc.json}) at the largest W0 weight on a grid of step \const{0.0005} whose mean gain keeps a non-negative nominal one-sided \const{95} percent lower bound, unadjusted for selection; estimated gain, that bound, and the mixture's efficiency, all on the evaluation records that selected the weight; ``Train gain'' is the mixture's mean gain on the training seeds (the registered W1 constraint requires it to be non-negative), reconstructed from the search archives. The row marked training-feasible, added after external review, uses the largest grid weight at which both the evaluation bound and the training gain are non-negative. B3's W0 attack has a positive estimated gain.}\label{tab:ph-welfare}
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:279:\paragraph{Secondary comparison.} At the headline market, with each rule's own tuned parameters, the gains are \site{r_sec_b3t} (B3-t), \site{r_sec_r3t} (R3-t), and \site{r_sec_cst} (C$^*$-t) under D2+H3. This comparison is secondary and descriptive.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:281:\paragraph{Search quality.} No cell is missing. The report lists the search-flagged cells, and the addendum traces the inherited flags of every Q1 and Q4 decision through the records' references. Q2 and Q3 statements are descriptive; the cells they use carry the flags listed in the report. Table~\ref{tab:ph-stability} sets the Q1 estimates beside their re-evaluation with the registered configurations, and Table~\ref{tab:ph-q4-stability} compares the Q4 estimates, calibrated lower bounds, and decisions under both configurations and gives the adjusted market $p$-value under the registered configuration; the largest Q4 change is on fresh4, where the gain difference moves from \site{r_st_f4_old} to \site{r_st_f4_new}, still above the registered \const{0.10}.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:293:\paragraph{Predictions.} Tables~\ref{tab:predictions} and~\ref{tab:predictions-b} score every registered prediction from the report. For Q4-b, the original scoring script compared the report's raw component $p$-values, although the prediction is registered on calibrated values; the corrected script compares the calibrated values, \site{r_pred_q4b_g} against \site{r_pred_q4b_e} on each of fresh0, fresh2, and fresh5. The prediction remains fulfilled and the total is unchanged: a scoring correction with no change of verdict, separate from the corrected labels of Tables~\ref{tab:q1} and~\ref{tab:q4}. The original numerical scoring path, preserved in the released scoring output, retains estimates from completed searches even when their doubling checks were marked incomplete; it counts \site{r_pred_n_ful} predictions fulfilled and \site{r_pred_n_fail} failed. A separate eligibility analysis applies the two filters below. The registered scoring rule also says that an incomplete target, in the sense of the design's search-quality checks, is not assessed; those checks call a search-flagged cell incomplete while keeping its estimate for the prescribed search procedure; the rule does not say whether a prediction computed from a flagged search is itself incomplete. Table~\ref{tab:pred-eligibility} adds two post hoc eligibility readings. Starting from the listed inputs of each scored comparison, we propagate flags through recorded upstream links, including references, attack banks, and warm starts. An inherited flag identifies a potentially relevant ancestor; it does not establish that the flagged candidate was selected or that the statistic would change. The map is released with the scorer, and neither sensitivity is designated as the official retrospective score. If a flagged required search makes a Q3 policy comparison ineligible, Q3-a, Q3-b, Q3-d, and Q3-e are not assessed: \site{r_pred_q_ful} predictions are fulfilled, \site{r_pred_q_fail} fail, and \site{r_pred_q_na} are not assessed (counting direct inputs only, Q3-e stays fulfilled: \site{r_pred_qd_ful}, \site{r_pred_qd_fail}, and \site{r_pred_qd_na}). The broader sensitivity excludes flagged scored cells outside six decision-related predictions retained by convention: Q1-a, its alternative, Q1-b, Q1-d, Q4-a, and Q4-b (the design keeps the decisions but does not say how predictions about them are scored). A prediction still fails if any eligible cell fails; otherwise, an excluded required cell leaves it not assessed. Q1-c and Q2-a to Q2-d are then also not assessed: \site{r_pred_d_ful} fulfilled, \site{r_pred_d_fail} failed, and \site{r_pred_d_na} not assessed (counting direct inputs only: \site{r_pred_dd_ful}, \site{r_pred_dd_fail}, and \site{r_pred_dd_na}). No reading turns a failed prediction into a fulfilled one or changes a registered decision; the marked rows of Tables~\ref{tab:predictions} and~\ref{tab:predictions-b} are those a stricter reading changes, and their estimates stand as estimates of the prescribed searches. The predictions differ in stakes and are not independent, so the count of fulfilled predictions is not a validation score. The failures are informative: we expected the priority phase to matter little, the replication to hold on at least five markets, X5-AG/v2 to stay below D2+H3, and the unconstrained welfare attack to leave R3's and C$^*$'s efficiency at the registered level, and none of these held (the first is not assessed under the broader reading).
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:297:\caption{Prediction tally under three eligibility readings, added after external review. Numerical: the scorer's statuses (Tables~\ref{tab:predictions} and~\ref{tab:predictions-b}). Flagged inputs, Q3: a Q3 cell with a search-flagged required search, direct or through the recorded dependencies of its evaluation record, is not assessed. Flagged inputs, all non-exempt cells: a flagged scored cell is excluded in every prediction except six decision-related predictions retained by convention; a prediction fails if any eligible cell fails. An inherited flag marks a potentially relevant ancestor, not a demonstrated change. The last two rows apply the same filters to the listed inputs of each cell only, without tracing. Eighteen scored predictions, including the alternative to Q1-a.}\label{tab:pred-eligibility}
paper/main.tex:153:\caption{Registered verdict of the first test (unchanged), on \evone{40} fresh seeds at $\beta = \evone{0.05}$ and the median position. Bounds are one-sided: \evone{95}\% for the rule clause and the absolute part, \evone{98.75}\% for the comparisons. The amended pass also requires the H2 rule clause and admission of the comparison bounds, whose interval procedures were admitted on development distributions with B3 as proxy; nominal coverage for the selected R3 is not established.}\label{tab:verdict1}
paper/main.tex:163:\paragraph{Questions.} Q1: does R3 ($F_{111}$) leave a lower or higher identity-replacement gain than C$^*$, at the fixed pair? Q2: what do the priority phase, the residual targeting, and the pacing contribute, alone and together? Q3: do the results hold against X7-SC, X5-AG/v2, the defence-aware sealed family H4, welfare attacks, and coalitions, and does R4 repair the reserve collapse? Q4: does the first test's comparison of B3 and R3 under D2+H3 replicate on fresh markets? Q1 and Q4 are confirmatory; Q2 and Q3 are descriptive.
paper/main.tex:176:Q1 & $d = g_{C^*} - g_{F_{111}}$, pooled, for D2+H3 and X7-SC. Direction: Holm's procedure~\cite{holm1979simple} on calibrated two-sided $p$-values for $d = \const{0}$. Size: the calibrated Bonferroni interval $I$ for $d$. Categories, in order: \emph{materially lower} if $I$ lies above $\const{0.05}$ (R3 lower) or below $\const{-0.05}$ (C$^*$ lower); \emph{equivalent} if $I$ lies within $[\const{-0.05}, \const{0.05}]$, with a direction label when Holm rejects $d = \const{0}$; \emph{ordered, size not established} if Holm rejects and neither applies; otherwise \emph{inconclusive}. A rule is called better only with the first category in its favour and a paired gross-efficiency difference (that rule minus the other) whose nominal one-sided \const{97.5}\% lower bound is at least $\const{-0.01}$ (descriptive; no calibrated joint guarantee). If that lower bound is below $\const{-0.01}$, the gain category stands and the result is labelled ``efficiency non-inferiority unresolved''; it is labelled ``lower gain with an efficiency loss'' only if the corresponding upper bound is below $\const{-0.01}$. \\
paper/main.tex:178:Q2, Q3 & Registered analyses descriptive, with nominal intervals and extrema selected on training data; the post hoc lottery analysis selects its companion and weight on evaluation data. \\\bottomrule
paper/main.tex:189:The registered estimates and decisions below come from the report that the tagged code wrote; the addendum beside it (Section~\ref{sec:design}) adds labels and disclosures. Tables~\ref{tab:ph-q1}, \ref{tab:ph-q4}, \ref{tab:ph-welfare}, and~\ref{tab:ph-welfare-sens} report exploratory analyses of existing records, and Tables~\ref{tab:ph-stability} and~\ref{tab:ph-q4-stability} the separate registered-configuration stability check; none of these changes a registered estimate or decision. Gains are normalised attacker gains; intervals are those of Table~\ref{tab:rules-v2}. Some descriptive fixed-reference contrasts on fresh1 (\site{r_ext_n} interval entries in the report's per-market Q2 rows) have numerically extreme studentized intervals: the reference-profit contrast there is above rounding scale on only \site{r_ext_seeds} of \site{r_ext_total} seeds (the others are at most \site{r_ext_round} in absolute value), and \site{r_ext_cnt} of the \site{r_ext_tot} frozen resamples (\site{r_ext_share}) omit all three, so their standard error is of rounding size and their bootstrap-t statistics are huge. We retain these frozen outputs, list them in the audit file, and draw no inferential conclusion from them; no other interval in the report has a bound beyond $\const{1000}$ in absolute value.
paper/main.tex:191:\paragraph{Age-ranked versus history-based checking (Q1).} Pooled over the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches leave the identity-replacement attacker a measured gain against C$^*$ higher than against R3 by \site{r_q1_d_est} under D2+H3 and by \site{r_q1_x_est} under X7-SC. Both direction tests reject $d = \const{0}$ (calibrated input $p' = $ \site{r_q1_d_p}; Holm-adjusted $p = $ \site{r_q1_holm} for each). Both intervals lie inside the registered margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$: the categories are ``\site{r_q1_d_cat}'' (D2+H3) and ``\site{r_q1_x_cat}'' (X7-SC), and the separate efficiency label is ``\site{r_q1_d_eff}''. R3 is not materially better than the development-selected comparator. The difference changes sign across markets: per market (descriptive), it ranges from \site{r_q1_f3_d} on fresh3 to \site{r_q1_f1_d} on fresh1 under D2+H3. Both decisions are about the prescribed search procedure: the D2+H3 test uses search-flagged cells, and the X7-SC test uses them through its references (addendum); their nominal error guarantees are not established on the fresh, selected-rule distributions (Section~\ref{sec:design}). The paired descriptive differences in Table~\ref{tab:q1-side} are small for efficiency, audit spending, refunds on false audit failures, and the market service fraction; the reference-profit column is in raw profit units. Splitting $d$ with the same normalisation into the attacked-profit term and the reference-profit term (Table~\ref{tab:ph-q1}, exploratory) shows that almost all of it is attacked profit: the attacker earns more under C$^*$ when it deviates, not when it is truthful.
paper/main.tex:205:\caption{Q1 side evidence, R3 minus C$^*$, pooled over the fresh markets (descriptive).}\label{tab:q1-side}
paper/main.tex:213:\paragraph{Repeating the comparison on six new markets (Q4).} The registered B3--R3 gain-and-efficiency comparison under D2+H3 passes on \site{r_q4_k} of the six markets (Table~\ref{tab:q4}), and all three passes (fresh1, fresh3, fresh4) are about the prescribed search procedure. On every market the estimated B3 minus R3 gain difference is positive and R3 keeps its efficiency condition; where the comparison fails, the difference is smaller than the registered threshold, not reversed: \site{r_q4_f0} on fresh0, \site{r_q4_f2} on fresh2, and \site{r_q4_f5} on fresh5, against \site{r_q4_f1} on fresh1. R3's efficiency on fresh2, the lowest, is \site{r_q4_f2_eff}. The decisions on fresh0, fresh1, fresh3, and fresh4 are about the prescribed search procedure (fresh0 and fresh3 through their references; addendum). The registered prediction of at least five passing markets failed. Q4 does not test the complete amended claim of the first test. Table~\ref{tab:ph-q4} (exploratory) decomposes each market's gain difference, which is mostly attacked profit. R3's reported audit spending is about five percent of the realised first-best surplus, and the market's service fraction (all jobs served over the first-best number) is near one. The three non-passing markets have smaller attacked-profit reductions; these descriptive diagnostics do not identify their cause.
paper/main.tex:234:\caption{Q2 under D2+H3: factorial contrasts, searched and fixed attacks, with attack and reference terms and efficiency (descriptive, nominal intervals).}\label{tab:q2}
paper/main.tex:247:\paragraph{Stronger attacks and where R3 fails (Q3).} Table~\ref{tab:q3-envelope} gives each core rule's attack envelope: the family with the highest training gain, evaluated unchanged. These are finite-search results, not bounds on the families. Against B3 the selected envelope reaches \site{r_q3_f000_f1} on fresh1; against R3 it is \site{r_q3_f111_f1} there, and against C$^*$ \site{r_q3_cs_f1}, from \site{r_q3_cs_f1_fam}. H4 was written in a separate session against the frozen defenders and committed by its hash before the lock; its author's containment note states that it contains D2+H3 exactly as a special case, so it extends the public family rather than offering a disjoint attack concept. Evaluated at the headline market only, its selected search gains \site{r_q3_f111_h4} against R3 and \site{r_q3_f000_h4} against B3. Robustness against D2+H3 is unanswered for every core rule because of search flags, and so is R3's robustness against X7-SC; X5-AG/v2 and H4 carry no search-quality claim. Welfare attacks are the clearest weakness. At the headline market, the selected W0 attacks (no profit constraint) yield gross efficiency \site{r_q3_w0_f000} for B3 and \site{r_q3_w0} for R3, with gains relative to the truthful reference of \site{r_q3_w0g_f000} and \site{r_q3_w0g_f111}; C$^*$ is like R3. Under the prescribed W1 searches (mean gain at least zero), the selected feasible attacks yield efficiency \site{r_q3_w1} for R3, while B3 stays at \site{r_q3_w1_f000} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-welfare}). These finite-search results are not lower bounds on efficiency over a broader policy class. An episode-level mixture, which picks a welfare attack or a profitable attack at the start of each episode, satisfies a mean-gain constraint only when its probability-weighted component gains satisfy that constraint; we examine such mixtures on the existing evaluation records. On the records used to select them, W0 weights of \site{r_ph_w_r3} for R3 and \site{r_ph_w_cs} for C$^*$, with the H4 attack as companion, give estimated normalised gains of \site{r_ph_g_r3} and \site{r_ph_g_cs} and gross efficiencies of \site{r_ph_e_r3} and \site{r_ph_e_cs}; with the public D2+H3 attack as companion the efficiencies are \site{r_ph_e_r3_d} and \site{r_ph_e_cs_d} (Tables~\ref{tab:ph-welfare} and~\ref{tab:ph-welfare-sens}). The registered W1 constraint is a non-negative mean gain on the training seeds. Reconstructed from the search archives, the training mean gains of these mixtures, each at its rule--companion pair's weight in Table~\ref{tab:ph-welfare}, are \site{r_ph_t_r3} and \site{r_ph_t_r3_d} for R3 and \site{r_ph_t_cs} and \site{r_ph_t_cs_d} for C$^*$ (H4 and D2+H3 companions): three meet the training constraint, but C$^*$'s public-family mixture does not. At its evaluation-selected weight of \site{r_ph_wd_cs}, C$^*$'s public-family mixture fails the training constraint. At \site{r_ph_fw_cs}, the largest weight on the same grid at which both the training mean gain and the evaluation bound are non-negative, it has training gain \site{r_ph_ft_cs}, estimated evaluation gain \site{r_ph_fg_cs}, and gross efficiency \site{r_ph_fe_cs}, against \site{r_ph_w1_cs} under the registered W1 search (last row of Table~\ref{tab:ph-welfare}). Both rules therefore show substantial empirical efficiency losses with public-family mixtures alone; H4 is not needed for a drop of this size, and population profitability remains unestablished. The evaluation-selected weights are the largest at which a nominal one-sided \const{95} percent normal bound on the mean gain stays non-negative (the training-feasible row also requires a non-negative training mean); that bound is not adjusted for selection, the weight and the companion family were chosen on the evaluation seeds, and the bound is close to zero at the selected weights, so this checks the policy class and is not an out-of-sample estimate. The mixture efficiencies stay above \const{0.90}; the analysis does not touch Q4 or its registered predicate. For coalitions of two synchronised members, the selected total gains are \site{r_q3_coal_f000} against B3 and \site{r_q3_coal_f111_c2} against R3 (search flagged); for five, \site{r_q3_coal_f000_c5} and \site{r_q3_coal_f111} (total objective), while the policy selected for the minimum-member objective leaves R3's worst member a mean gain of \site{r_q3_coal_f111_c5min}; the decoy-and-beneficiary family gives \site{r_q3_db_f000} against B3 and \site{r_q3_db_f111} against R3. These comparisons establish neither coalition stability nor an ordering across every coalition family (Table~\ref{tab:q3-coalitions}). At $\rho = \const{1}$, where R3's terminal reserve collapses, R3 leaves \site{r_rep_f111} under D2+H3; the repair R4 lowers this to \site{r_rep_r4} and C$^*$ with the capped schedule (C$^*$/S2) to \site{r_rep_cs2} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-repair}).
paper/main.tex:250:\caption{Exploratory, after the report: at the headline market, the efficiency of the selected W1 and W0 attacks, and, for each companion family, the episode-level lottery between the W0 attack and a profitable attack of the same rule (two displayed companions, H4 and D2+H3; all five examined companions are in \texttt{posthoc.json}) at the largest W0 weight on a grid of step \const{0.0005} whose mean gain keeps a non-negative nominal one-sided \const{95} percent lower bound, unadjusted for selection; estimated gain, that bound, and the mixture's efficiency, all on the evaluation records that selected the weight; ``Train gain'' is the mixture's mean gain on the training seeds (the registered W1 constraint requires it to be non-negative), reconstructed from the search archives. The row marked training-feasible, added after external review, uses the largest grid weight at which both the evaluation bound and the training gain are non-negative. B3's W0 attack has a positive estimated gain.}\label{tab:ph-welfare}
